      "exotic_ratio_at_min": 0.0005,
      "exotic_ratio_at_realistic": 0.02,
      "exotic_ratio_at_max": 5
    },
    "orbital_spacing": {
      "name": "Planet spacing. The RATIO of successive orbits is the rule, drawn once per system and varied slightly per gap: that is what is near-constant in real systems (Sol averages about 1.7, TRAPPIST-1 about 1.32), while their separations in mutual Hill radii vary eightfold because the planet masses do. Scale-free, so it carries nothing about Sol to another star - unlike the Titius-Bode block this replaced, which was Sol's own orbits in absolute AU. Mutual Hill radii are kept as the STABILITY FLOOR under the ratio (Pu & Wu 2015: roughly 10 for gigayear survival), which is also what keeps the slots either side of a massive body clear.",
